**What**

`comment edit <id> --edit <text>` writes a `CommentEdit { id, edit }` to
`refs/forge/comment/<id>`, keyed by the *issue* id, using the same
commit-forward snapshot path as everything else. There is exactly one comment
record per entity and the second one overwrites the first. There is no
`comment new`, no `comment list`, no `comment show`; the only reader is
`comment log`, which prints commit oids and timestamps but not the text.
`issue show` never displays comments at all.

The field is called `edit` and the interactive prompt for it is literally
`Edit reason`, so this was never trying to be a comment -- it is an audit
annotation that nothing reads.

**Where**

- `crates/git-forge/src/main.rs:118` -- `CommentCommand` has only `Edit` and `Log`
- `crates/git-forge/src/main.rs:615` -- `prompt_comment_edit_reason`, prompt `Edit reason`
- `crates/gix-forge/src/lib.rs:216` -- `CommentEdit { id, edit }`
- `crates/gix-forge/src/lib.rs:454` and `:465` -- `Commentable::add_comment` and
  `add_anchored_comment` are TODO stubs that return `ObjectId::null` and throw
  away the `comments_ref()` they just computed
- `crates/gix-forge/src/lib.rs:484` -- `get_comments` calls `Comments::open(repo).list(None)`
  and ignores the entity entirely, so every entity returns every comment in the repo
- `crates/gix-forge/src/lib.rs:524` -- `comments_ref` builds `refs/forge/issues/<id>/comments`,
  plural, which does not match the `refs/forge/issue/<id>` the store actually uses

**Also**

    git-forge comment edit zzzznope --edit "orphan comment"
    e68af9c8...      # exit 0

No check that the entity exists. Orphan comment refs are creatable at will.

**Why it matters**

A forge is a place where people argue about code. Issues you cannot reply to
are a bug tracker for one person. This is not an unimplemented feature -- the
data model has nowhere to put a second comment, so it is a design gap, and it
is the first thing anyone will notice in a demo.

It is also the place where the family thesis is most obviously on the table and
untaken. `gix-comment` and `gix-anchor` are already dependencies. The
`Commentable` trait sketches exactly the right shape: a comment that anchors to
a line range in a blob and survives the code moving. A review comment pinned to
moving code is a thing GitHub does badly and `git-anchor` does well. The stubs
are three functions away from being the best slide in the talk.

**Options**

- Option A -- Make comments an anonymous entity kind of their own
  (`store_anonymous("comment", ...)` with a `parent` field), so each comment
  gets its own ref and id. Threads are then a filter over the kind, and
  concurrent commenting merges for free because ids never collide. Trade-off:
  one ref per comment, and thread assembly is a scan.
- Option B -- Store a thread as an append-only list in one ref per entity, so
  `refs/forge/comment/<entity-id>` grows rather than being overwritten.
  Trade-off: cheaper reads, but concurrent comments from two clones collide on
  the same ref -- the same merge problem filed separately.
- Option C -- Delegate to `gix-comment` and `gix-anchor` outright: implement
  the `Commentable` stubs, bind comments to the entity tree oid for thread
  comments and to a blob line range for inline ones, and let `git-anchor` do
  the surviving-a-rebase work. Trade-off: a real dependency on a sibling that
  is itself a sketch.

**Recommendation**

A for the plain thread, C for inline review comments, and delete the
`edit` / `CommentEdit` concept entirely -- it is a third thing that is neither.
High confidence. Of everything here this is the largest gap between what the
tagline promises and what the binary does, and Option C is the highest-upside
work in the repo.